        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111:\n\nAfter the first edge addition:\n\nColoring node 111 red, node 222 blue: 12⋅13=16\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{6}21​⋅31​=61​\nColoring node 111 blue, node 222 red: 12⋅13=16\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{6}21​⋅31​=61​\nThe colors of nodes 333 and 444 don't matter since they aren't adjacent to anything.\nSo, the probability that the coloring is proper, is 16+16=13=332748118(mod998244353)\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}=332748118\\pmod {998244353}61​+61​=31​=332748118(mod998244353)\n\n\nAfter the second edge addition:\n\nNode 111 red, node 222 blue, node 333 red: 12⋅13⋅24=112\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{1}{12}21​⋅31​⋅42​=121​\nNode 111 blue, node 222 red, Node 333 blue: 12⋅13⋅24=112\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{1}{12}21​⋅31​⋅42​=121​\nThe color of node 444 doesn't matter.\nSo, the probability of the coloring being proper is 112+112=16=166374059(mod998244353)\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{1}{6}=166374059\\pmod {998244353}121​+121​=61​=166374059(mod998244353)\n\n\nAfter the fourth edge addition, there doesn't exist a way to properly color the graph using only red and blue. So, the probability is 000."
      }
